On Sun, Jun 9, 2013 at 8:30 AM, Bob LeChevalier, President and Founder -
LLG <lojbab@lojban.org> wrote:

>
> And dotside or no dotside, I will never accept someone's choice of name to
> be "la" %^)
>
>
Dotside or no dotside, the word "la" cannot be a name by itself (although
it can be part of a name, as in "la broda be la brode", where the second
"la" is a part of the name, but that has nothing to do with dotside).

All dotside does is remove the requirement for cmevla not to contain the
syllables doi-la-lai-la'i. It does not allow anything else to be names.
Dotside adds the requirement that cmevla that begin with a consonant must
always be preceded by a dot. It was always the case that cmevla that begin
with a vowel must be preceded by a dot and it was always the case that all
cmevla must always end with a dot, It was also always the case that all
cmevla required to be preceded by a dot in some contexts, for example when
quoted with "zo". It was always "zo .lojbab." that's not a new requirement
of dotside.

For some reason, people seem to have very strange ideas about what dotside
is. It certainly does not mean that you can take any word, put dots around
it, and you end up with a name. It also does not mean that you need dots
around any name. You only need them around cmevla.

mu'o mi'e xorxes

